<div>Dear Robert Samuel White:<br />
<br />
Thank you for your interest in public records of the Oregon Secretary of St=
ate. Your request has been received and is being processed in accordance wi=
th the Oregon Public Records Law. Your request was received on 10/27/2026 a=
nd given the reference number R000687-102726 for tracking purposes.<br />
<br />
<strong>Records Requested:</strong> To the Public Records Officer, Oregon S=
ecretary of State:<br><br>Under ORS 192.314, I request the following public=
 records of the Secretary of State, including the Archives Division and its=
 Records Management Unit. **This request excludes any record concerning me.=
** It concerns how the State Archivist has told agencies to keep and find t=
heir email, and what the Archives knew about the state's move of every exec=
utive branch mailbox to one system.<br><br>**Background.** The State Archiv=
ist approves every state agency's records policy under ORS 192.018 and sets=
 the retention schedules in OAR chapter 166.[^1] The Archives authorized ag=
encies to apply role-based retention to electronic communications, includin=
g email.[^2] The Oregon Records Management Solution, offered through the Ar=
chives, has been described by its vendor as fulfilling "a three-year email =
request" in "90 seconds with only ~250 messages needing manual review."[^3]=
 Enterprise Information Services decommissioned the state's on-premises ema=
il on November 21, 2024, after moving 46 agencies to one Microsoft 365 plat=
form.[^4]<br><br>**Records requested.** For the period January 1, 2023, to =
the date of search, unless stated otherwise:<br><br>1. Every guidance docum=
ent, bulletin, FAQ, training material, or communication the Archives Divisi=
on has issued to state agencies since January 1, 2023 on retaining, managin=
g, or searching email as a public record, including the role-based retentio=
n authorization and any guidance on locating email responsive to a public r=
ecords request.<br>2. Every communication since January 1, 2023 between the=
 Archives Division and the Department of Administrative Services, Enterpris=
e Information Services, or the Office of the State Chief Information Office=
r concerning the Microsoft 365 migration, the retention of email on the ent=
erprise tenant, the application of retention schedules to Exchange Online m=
ailboxes, or the use of Microsoft 365 eDiscovery, content search, or retent=
ion policies.<br>3. The list of public bodies using the Oregon Records Mana=
gement Solution as of the date of search, and every record describing the S=
olution's capture, indexing, or search of email, including any statement of=
 how long an email search takes.<br>4. Every record showing which state age=
ncies' email is captured into the Oregon Records Management Solution or ano=
ther Archives-managed system, and which is held only in the agency's mailbo=
xes on the enterprise Microsoft 365 tenant.<br>5. The Archives Division's o=
wn written procedure for searching its email in response to a public record=
s request: who performs the search, what tool is used, and how the time is =
recorded and charged.<br><br>If any of these records is kept in a system, a=
n export or report from that system is acceptable.<br><br>**The search.** P=
lease state in writing the accounts, systems, and archives searched; the ex=
act terms and date range used; the date of the search and the title of the =
person who conducted it; and the number of responsive records found. The st=
atute does not require you to describe your search. I am asking because the=
 search is the answer, and I would rather publish yours than my guess at it=
. If there are no responsive records for any item, please say so in writing=
, item by item, with the search described above.<br><br>**Format and corres=
pondence.** Under ORS 192.324(3), please provide records in their native el=
ectronic format with metadata intact, by email or secure link. Please condu=
ct all correspondence about this request by email, including acknowledgment=
s, estimates, waiver decisions, and payment instructions. Under ORS 192.324=
(1), this letter is a complete written request, and it includes a complete =
fee waiver request. It does not need to be resubmitted on a form.<br><br>**=
Redaction.** Please redact the names, home addresses, personal phone number=
s, and personal email addresses of private individuals. No other redaction =
is requested for privacy. In any fee estimate, please list redaction time s=
eparately from search and production time, with the hours and the hourly ra=
te of the employee performing it. Under ORS 192.324(4)(b), a fee may not in=
clude attorney time spent determining the application of the public records=
 law.<br><br>**Exemptions.** For any record or portion withheld, please ide=
ntify the record, the specific exemption claimed, and how it applies to tha=
t record (ORS 192.329, 192.338). Please segregate and release nonexempt por=
tions. A fee estimate is not an exemption assertion. Guidance, training mat=
erial, and advice to agencies on how to search for public records are not a=
ttorney-client communications by virtue of having been written by a lawyer;=
 if you claim privilege for any record, please identify the client, the att=
orney, and the legal advice sought.<br><br>**Fee waiver.** I request a waiv=
er of all fees under ORS 192.324(5). Disclosure primarily benefits the gene=
ral public:<br><br>1. *Requester's identity.* I maintain oprdvolunteerabuse=
.org, a public archive of Oregon state government records. It has published=
 complete public records productions from the Oregon State Police, Departme=
nt of Administrative Services, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department, and =
the Office of the Public Records Advocate.[^A] This request excludes any re=
cord concerning me.<br>2. *Purpose.* To publish, in full and without charge=
, the guidance Oregon's public bodies are given on searching email when the=
 public asks for it, next to what they tell the public it costs.<br>3. *Cha=
racter of the information.* I am asking every state agency in Oregon the sa=
me question about its email: what did it write to the State Police press of=
fice, and about whom. The first of those requests went out on October 22 an=
d October 27, 2026. Before the day was out, one agency had its twelve staff=
 searching their own inboxes,[^B] and another was drawing up an invoice for=
 "many hours of staff labor"[^C] for a search the state can run in minutes =
on a system it already owns.[^D] The Archives set the rules every agency ke=
eps its email under, and its own records system is sold on a ninety-second =
email search. The records I am asking for show what the Archives told the a=
gencies, and which agencies have the tool. That is the administration of OR=
S 192.311 to 192.478, and it is the fee the public is charged under ORS 192=
.324(4).<br>4. *Public domain.* The retention schedules and the role-based =
authorization are published. What the Archives told agencies about finding =
email, what it said to EIS about the migration, and which agencies capture =
email into a searchable system are not.<br>5. *Ability to disseminate.* The=
 archive has published every production it has received, with original file=
s and metadata, at no charge. Records from this request will be published t=
he same way. I have no commercial interest in them.<br><br>If a full waiver=
 is denied, I request a reduction of fees under the same provision. If the =
waiver is denied or a reduction is granted in part, please give the reasons=
 in writing for each factor, state the amount or percentage of any reductio=
n and its basis, and provide an itemized estimate before any work begins, s=
howing the tasks, the hours for each, and the hourly rate of the employee p=
erforming each.<br><br>**Timeline.** Please acknowledge this request within=
 five business days and give an estimated completion date as required by OR=
S 192.324 and 192.329.<br><br>**Preservation.** Please preserve all records=
 responsive to this request while it and any petition for review are pendin=
g.<br><br>[^1]: ORS 192.018; OAR 166-030-0026, Public Records Retention and=
 Disposition Authorization (State Agencies). https://www.law.cornell.edu/re=
gulations/oregon/Or-Admin-Code-SS-166-030-0026<br>[^2]: Oregon State Archiv=
es, "Role Based Retention Authorization." https://sos.oregon.gov/archives/D=
ocuments/Role-Based-Retention-Authorization.pdf<br>[^3]: Carahsoft, "State =
of Oregon achieves fast, cost-effective public records management," case st=
udy naming the Director of the State Archives Division. https://www.casestu=
dies.com/company/carahsoft/case-study/state-of-oregon-customer-case-study<b=
r>[^4]: EIS Digest, December 2024. https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts=
/ORDAS/bulletins/3c6649e<br>[^A]: Oregon State Police, PR27478, https://opr=
dvolunteerabuse.org/records/osp/; Department of Administrative Services, ht=
tps://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/records/das/; Oregon Parks and Recreation Depa=
rtment, https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/records/oprd/; Office of the Public =
Records Advocate, October 27, 2026, https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/g=
overnor/01M5ZTYWMR1EM9C7R3S815Z4FG/<br>[^B]: Employment Relations Board, Oc=
tober 27, 2026. https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/public-question-1/01M=
5ZMSTZG2CY6QT8SJTNGPSQW/<br>[^C]: Oregon Department of Emergency Management=
, October 27, 2026. https://oprdvolunteerabuse.org/record/public-question-1=
/01M5ZPMTNRMPG3CRR9XYTPXM0H/<br>[^D]: Microsoft 365 Progress Report, Joint =
Ways and Means Subcommittee on General Government, February 13, 2020, pp. 4=
 and 11 ("Advanced Compliance: Advanced eDiscovery"), https://olis.oregonle=
gislature.gov/liz/2020R1/Downloads/CommitteeMeetingDocument/216317; EIS Dig=
est, December 2024 (on-premises email decommissioned November 21, 2024; 46 =
agencies), https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/ORDAS/bulletins/3c6649e=
; EIS NTT Pro Services Special Procurement, June 10, 2025 (eDiscovery among=
 supported M365 applications), https://www.highergov.com/sl/contract-opport=
unity/or-eis-ntt-pro-services-special-procurement-51980448/<br><br>Respectf=
ully,<br><br>**Robert Samuel White**<br />
<br />
Your request will be forwarded to the relevant division(s) to locate the in=
formation you seek and to determine the volume and any costs associated wit=
h satisfying your request. You will be notified when your records are avail=
able or if we need further information from you. PLEASE NOTE: The Oregon Pu=
blic Records Law&nbsp;does not require a governmental body to create new in=
formation, to do legal research, or to answer questions.<br />
<br />
You can monitor the progress of your request at the link below and you'll r=
eceive an email when your request has been completed. Again, thank you for =
using the Public Records Request Center.<br />
<br />
Oregon Secretary of State</div>
